Diabetic Neuropathic Pain
Musculoskeletal & Pain
Symmetrical stocking-glove distribution, Burning nocturnal pain, Loss of protective sensation

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MKP-018
Reason for Visit: Severe, agonizing nocturnal burning pain in the bilateral lower extremities preventing any sleep and causing extreme psychological distress.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified an elderly diabetic patient experiencing an acute, severe neuro-inflammatory pain crisis in the legs that standard analgesics could not touch. Urgent assessment was required to rule out acute limb ischemia or rapidly progressing foot infection, and to administer specialized neuropathic medications to halt the pain cycle and allow for restorative sleep prior to daytime services.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My feet feel like they are submerged in boiling water; the pain is relentless, and even the bedsheets feel like razor blades."
History of Present Illness: Patient with a 15-year history of Type II Diabetes Mellitus presents with an acute exacerbation of bilateral lower extremity pain. Timeline: Long-standing mild numbness in the toes over the past 2 years; evolved over the last week into a severe, constant burning and lancinating pain; culminated tonight in an agonizing flare that is completely unmanageable. The pain exhibits a symmetrical stocking-and-glove distribution. Profound nocturnal exacerbation is noted, completely disrupting sleep. Patient reports severe hyperesthesia and allodynia, actively refusing to let blankets touch the feet.
Functional Status: Bedbound, actively holding feet outside the covers, terrified of friction.
Social/Safety Concerns: Severe sleep deprivation and high fall risk due to worsening sensory ataxia and loss of protective sensation.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 152/88 | HR: 94 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 95 Kg
General: Alert, diaphoretic, deeply exhausted, rubbing the distal shins but avoiding touching the feet.
Physical Exam: Bilateral lower extremities demonstrate a symmetrical stocking distribution of distal sensory impairment. Severe hyperesthesia present; light touch with a cotton swab induces extreme grimacing. Loss of protective sensation confirmed via the 10-gram Semmes-Weinstein monofilament test on the plantar surfaces. Vibratory sense is completely absent at the distal interphalangeal joints of the hallux bilaterally. Achilles deep tendon reflexes are absent. Trophic changes noted: skin is dry and cracked with an absence of pedal hair. Distal pulses (DP and PT) are palpable bilaterally. Capillary refill <2 seconds. No open ulcers, localized erythema, or purulent discharge.
Environmental Observations: Patient's bedroom is cluttered; high risk of stepping on unseen objects given the profound sensory loss.
Medication Reconciliation: Taking regular Metformin. Has taken Paracetamol and Codeine tonight with absolutely zero relief.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute flare of Severe Diabetic Sensorimotor Polyneuropathy - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute Limb Ischemia - Ruled out clinically by the presence of palpable distal pulses, normal capillary refill, and bilateral symmetrical presentation rather than a unilateral cold, pale limb.

Diabetic Foot Infection/Osteomyelitis - Ruled out by careful inspection revealing no open ulcerations, no localized erythema, and absence of systemic fever.

Lumbar Spinal Stenosis - Ruled out by the strictly distal, symmetrical "stocking" distribution of pain, rather than radicular pain radiating down the posterior thighs initiated by walking.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with a severe, sleep-depriving crisis of neuropathic pain, characteristic of advanced diabetic polyneuropathy. The acute allodynia and burning sensation rendered standard opiate analgesia completely ineffective, leaving the patient in a state of extreme distress and sleep deprivation. Delaying assessment until the next in-hours period would have subjected the patient to unmanageable suffering and risked missing a limb-threatening ischemic event or silent infection in a high-risk diabetic foot. Urgent after-hours evaluation safely excluded acute surgical vascular emergencies and occult infections, establishing the purely neuropathic nature of the crisis. This allowed for the immediate initiation of targeted membrane-stabilizing pharmacology to interrupt the aberrant nerve signaling and provide crucial relief.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered oral Pregabalin 75mg STAT. Prescribed Pregabalin 75mg Nocté ongoing. Strongly advised the cessation of codeine, as it is ineffective for this specific pain mechanism and causes constipation.
Positive Behavioral Change:
Action: Use a bed cradle or place a firm box at the foot of the bed to tent the blankets over the feet. Consequence: Complete elimination of unpredictable fabric friction against the hypersensitive skin, instantly stopping the sharp, allodynic pain spikes and allowing sleep (negative reinforcement).
Action: Apply a thick, unscented emollient cream to the heels and soles every morning. Consequence: Restoration of the skin barrier, preventing the deep, painful cracks and fissures that often occur with neuropathic dry skin (positive reinforcement).
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral to High-Risk Podiatry via the primary GP.
DME/Safety (Optional): Recommended immediate clearing of bedroom floor clutter to prevent silent injuries.
Follow-up: Routine GP review within 3-5 days to review neuropathic medication efficacy, arrange an HbA1c test, and coordinate a comprehensive diabetic review.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if one foot suddenly turns completely white, cold, and blue, if a high fever develops, or if any black, foul-smelling spots appear on the toes.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The diagnosis is clinical, based on a known diabetic history. Tight glycemic control is the only way to prevent further nerve destruction. Conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: Neuropathic pain requires specific medications that take several days to build up in the system. The burning will slowly decrease, but the numbness is likely permanent.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek emergency medical care immediately if you notice any new cuts or sores that look infected, or if one foot suddenly becomes freezing cold and changes color.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The high blood sugars over the years have damaged the insulation on the longest nerves in your body. They are now misfiring and sending false "burning" signals to your brain. Standard painkillers like Panadeine don't work for this. The new medication (Pregabalin) works directly on the nerves to turn down the volume of these false alarms.
Actionable Steps: Build the blanket tent tonight. Do not let anything touch the feet. Tomorrow, you must check the bottom of your feet with a mirror; you no longer have the protective sensation required to feel a cut or a tack in your shoe.

